6f276912bd5d29ff10eab269c08edbedc5808159f7d8a79c3fe9ed8f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113537" :question/id #uuid "148e2745-4d95-5c97-ae79-9141174f3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-113537" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113537/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113537"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113533/selection/7a6c82c3fa31f98b4b6ba0c1db2808e7bb76f6091a3188998ad4048b2b734b31" :answer/id #uuid "bf0fdccd-b602-5e49-9a18-3e6a4dad345a" :answer/type :