-b1c4-6d6aa37e868f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113533" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113533/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113533"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113544/selection/b3e79e9dbf17b625bd37eac2e092c3537cd3524279448c81da6763a2bc82d1fb" :answer/id #uuid "a07e2e9d-f1d6-5417-9ac8-4a49771bf319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113544/selection/03966356686f8d8d51444a62c90ba4d9d41695c4db11538c18a2f282d5257172" :