nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-49360/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49360"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49524"] :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2+4x}+\\frac{1}{2-4x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2+4x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2+4x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
